fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1116", description: some(value: "anthill-todo backend, INCREMENT 4 of WI-437: COORDINATED ADD. Design: backend-github-coordination.md §6.1, §6.2, §6.3, §6.4, with §8.1; §14 row 4. Ships conflict-free AND collision-free `add`, online or off -- directories alone (increment 2) fix conflicts but not id collisions, which is why coordination is not optional. STAKE BY CREATION (§6.1): a claim on an id is staked by CREATING the issue already titled `WI-<n>:`. Creation is atomic and totally ordered by issue number, so lowest-number-wins is sound; the earlier stake-by-retitle CAS was abandoned because it had an unfixable both-keep interleaving. Registry reads use the issues LIST endpoint with --state all -- the search index serves ONLY the old-claimant leg, it is not live enough for the race -- and claims are parsed strictly as a `^WI-<n>:` title prefix. Losing a race means retreat (§6.1: retitle + close) and retry. THE SPEC CHANGES IN EXACTLY TWO PLACES (§8.1): `next_id(s)` becomes `alloc_id(s, summary)` with effects {Modify[s], External, Error}, and External joins `commit`'s row (its coordinated body writes the GithubIssue fact and runs the best-effort tail). Allocation is the one thing the two backends do DIFFERENTLY IN KIND rather than differently in mechanism -- a counter bump versus an optimistic retried stake -- so it is the thing the interface has to be honest about; every other operation keeps its signature. AUTONOMOUS MODE (§6.4): an offline or unauthenticated `add` mints a PROVISIONAL id `WI-t<6hex>` from host entropy (fresh_token, increment 3) and proceeds -- Disabled access removes the synchronous attempt, never the work. Reconciliation is increment 5's job. Also: `GithubIssue` facts (new, in the bundled domain next to the backend entities, keyed on the work-item id -- the same additive shape as Tag, so the stage0 domain stays backend-neutral and a local project never sees the field). COUNTER SEEDING DISAPPEARS (§8.3): main.rs today scans the KB for the max WI-NNN to seed the WIS cell's id_counter, and under coordination that seed is exactly the §1.2 bug -- alloc_id reads the registry, provisional ids come from entropy. §6.3 names what can still go wrong and what repairs it (increment 5)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1114", "WI-1115"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: ProposalRejected(reason: "Retired unbuilt 2026-08-17. ContentHash (WI-1121, design §6.5) supersedes stake-by-creation: ids are minted locally from author + created + description, so GitHub coordinates nothing and this protocol has no job left. Its one irreplaceable property -- a registry visible to a STALE OR SHALLOW checkout, since local minting leaves no trace outside the tree -- is a nicety, because ContentHash handles collisions by detection and convergent repair (§6.6) rather than by prevention. That is ~250 lines of retry loop, lost-race retreat, provisional ids and reconciliation for a nicety, and retiring spec that was never implemented costs nothing: no migration, no deprecation, no users. The analysis stays on the page as design §6.0-§6.4, marked NOT PLANNED -- §6.1's both-keep interleaving, which killed an earlier retitle-CAS design, is a real result and would have to be rediscovered by anyone attempting forge-side allocation again. MirrorEntry facts move to WI-1117, where the mirror is.", at: "2026-08-17T09:44:55Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1116", name: "wi437")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1116", author: "user", content: "SCOPE CHANGED 2026-08-17, READ BEFORE CLAIMING. Allocation is now a POLICY (design §3.2, §6.5, filed as WI-1121), and this ticket is one of two implementations rather than the only one.\n\nWhat this ticket now is: the StakeByCreation policy -- §6.1's stake-by-creation protocol, §6.4's provisional fallback and their reconciliation. What it no longer is: the way ids get allocated. WI-1121's ContentHash policy mints ids locally from the item's author, created timestamp and description, needs no network, and closes the §1 id-collision half on its own. So this is no longer on the critical path to WI-437's shipping value, and rows 3-5 of §14 come off that path with it.\n\nMirrorEntry facts move to WI-1117, where the mirror is -- they are a mirror concern, and under ContentHash there is no allocation event to record.\n\nAND §12 NOW ASKS WHETHER TO BUILD THIS AT ALL. It is strictly more machinery for a property nothing in the tracker requires: a dense, globally ordered id sequence. Density is unused; ordering is recoverable from the created timestamp WI-1121 adds. The ONE thing it offers that no local policy can is a registry visible to a STALE OR SHALLOW CHECKOUT -- allocation under ContentHash leaves no trace outside the tree. That is a real property, and it is the whole case for this ticket. Weigh it after living on ContentHash for a while, not before. Deleting this later is cheap precisely because allocation is a policy now.\n\nIf you do claim it: §6.0 renamed from §6's opening, and §6.1-§6.4 are unchanged and still accurate.", at: "2026-08-17T08:43:06Z")
